.TH LMFS 4
.SH NAME
lmfs, aux/llama \- a language model served as files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lmfs
[
.B -Dc
] [
.B -b
.I bytes
] [
.B -e
.I engine
] [
.B -l
.I dir
] [
.B -m
.I mtpt
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
] [
.I model
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lmfs
serves a language model's files on
.I mtpt
.RB ( /mnt/lm
by default, or none if only
.B -s
is given), and posts itself as
.BI /srv/ srvname
if
.B -s
is given.
Its files and their answers are those of
.IR lmsim (4),
which describes them:
.BR models ,
.BR clone ,
and each session's
.BR ctl ,
.BR data ,
.B top
and
.BR embed .
A model is a GGUF file in
.I dir
.RB ( /lib/lm ),
named for the file without
.BR .gguf ;
a model's package adds its file there.
A session starts with
.IR model ,
or the first of
.BR models .
.PP
The model runs in
.BR aux/llama ,
.IR engine 's
default, a program of llama.cpp's on ggml's WebGPU backend on
.IR gpu (3)
where the browser has a GPU, and else, or with
.BR -c ,
on ggml's CPU backend, in one thread.
On the GPU it works out each token's bits and rank there, and reads back those alone.
.I Lmfs
starts one for a model's tokenizer and one for the model, and talks to each on a pipe,
a request a line, so the model's work runs beside the server and never holds up
another session's open, read or
.BR ctl .
It loads a model once, for every session that names it.
.I Bytes
.RB ( -b ,
1 GB by default)
is what the engine may give the model, its caches and its scratch; the engine keeps
as many sequences as fit, and at least one.
.PP
.I Lmfs
cuts a text into parts as
.IR lmsim (4)
says, and scores each with the
.B ctx
tokens before it, and with the model's start-of-text token before the text's first.
It keeps each part's bits and ranks by the hash of its tokens, those before it,
the model's SHA-256, the backend and
.BR ctx ,
for 8 million tokens.
The engine keeps the model's state for the parts it scored last: a transformer's keys and
values, or a recurrent model's state every 256 tokens, so an edit inside a part scores again
only from the edit.
It decodes in blocks at fixed places, of 64 tokens on the CPU and 256 on the GPU,
so a part's bits do not depend on what it decoded before.
The GPU's bits differ from the CPU's by about 0.1 of a bit a token, on average.
A byte that is not UTF-8 is its byte's token.
.B Embed
gives the model's last hidden state, the mean over the span's tokens, of unit length.
.PP
.B Ctl
takes
.BR model ,
.B backend
.RB ( float ;
.B exact
is not built) and
.B ctx
(up to
.BR maxctx ,
8192), and
.BI digits " n\fR,"
the decimal places of
.BR data 's
and
.BR top 's
bits, 2 by default and 17 at most.
.BR Settings ,
for
.IR lmz 's
header, names the model, its SHA-256, the backend and the engine's pinned settings, not
.BR ctx .
.B Device
says where the model runs,
.B gpu
or
.BR cpu .
A read gives the
.B sha256
as
.B loading
until the engine has loaded the model.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
% pkg add qwen3-0.6b
% lmfs
% echo the cat sat on the mat. | bits
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B src/cmd/lmfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR lmsim (4),
.IR bits (1),
.IR pkg (1),
.B doc/words.md
.SH BUGS
.B Freq
is not served yet.
The engine scores one part at a time, not parts in batches.
On the CPU in wasm, a model of half a billion weights scores 5 to 15 tokens a second.
